.Dd June 30, 2026
.Dt MBT_RINGBUF 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm mbt_ringbuf_new ,
.Nm mbt_ringbuf_free ,
.Nm mbt_ringbuf_available ,
.Nm mbt_ringbuf_space ,
.Nm mbt_ringbuf_cap ,
.Nm mbt_ringbuf_produce ,
.Nm mbt_ringbuf_consume ,
.Nm mbt_ringbuf_discard ,
.Nm mbt_ringbuf_grow ,
.Nm mbt_ringbuf_shrink ,
.Nm mbt_ringbuf_update_peak ,
.Nm mbt_ringbuf_peak ,
.Nm mbt_ringbuf_write_ptr ,
.Nm mbt_ringbuf_write_done
.Nd dynamic ring buffer with bounded growth and shrink-on-idle
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In mbt/ringbuf.h
.Ft int
.Fn mbt_ringbuf_new "struct mbt_ringbuf **out" "uint32_t max_cap"
.Ft void
.Fn mbt_ringbuf_free "struct mbt_ringbuf *r"
.Ft uint32_t
.Fn mbt_ringbuf_available "const struct mbt_ringbuf *r"
.Ft uint32_t
.Fn mbt_ringbuf_space "const struct mbt_ringbuf *r"
.Ft uint32_t
.Fn mbt_ringbuf_cap "const struct mbt_ringbuf *r"
.Ft int
.Fn mbt_ringbuf_produce "struct mbt_ringbuf *r" "const void *src" "uint32_t len"
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn mbt_ringbuf_consume "struct mbt_ringbuf *r" "void *dst" "uint32_t len"
.Ft void
.Fn mbt_ringbuf_discard "struct mbt_ringbuf *r" "uint32_t len"
.Ft int
.Fn mbt_ringbuf_grow "struct mbt_ringbuf *r" "uint32_t want" "uint32_t *out_delta"
.Ft int
.Fn mbt_ringbuf_shrink "struct mbt_ringbuf *r" "uint32_t min_cap" "uint32_t *out_delta"
.Ft void
.Fn mbt_ringbuf_update_peak "struct mbt_ringbuf *r"
.Ft uint32_t
.Fn mbt_ringbuf_peak "const struct mbt_ringbuf *r"
.Ft uint8_t *
.Fn mbt_ringbuf_write_ptr "struct mbt_ringbuf *r" "uint32_t *out_len"
.Ft void
.Fn mbt_ringbuf_write_done "struct mbt_ringbuf *r" "uint32_t len"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Ao
.Vt struct mbt_ringbuf
.An
is a dynamic ring buffer that grows from 1 MSS (1460 bytes), doubling up to
.Fa max_cap .
When
.Fa max_cap
is 0, the buffer grows unboundedly.
.Pp
The buffer uses monotonic read/write counters with power-of-2 masking for
O(1) wrap-around.
No
.Fn memmove
is used during normal operation.
.Pp
.Fn mbt_ringbuf_new
allocates an empty ring buffer with maximum capacity
.Fa max_cap
(power-of-2 rounded internally).
Returns 0 on success, \-ENOMEM on failure.
.Fn mbt_ringbuf_free
frees the buffer.
.Pp
.Fn mbt_ringbuf_available
returns the number of bytes currently in the buffer.
.Fn mbt_ringbuf_space
returns available write space.
.Fn mbt_ringbuf_cap
returns the current allocated capacity.
.Pp
.Fn mbt_ringbuf_produce
copies
.Fa len
bytes from
.Fa src
into the buffer, growing it if necessary.
Returns 0 on success.
Returns \-ENOBUFS if at maximum capacity and data cannot fit.
Returns \-ENOMEM on allocation failure.
.Pp
.Fn mbt_ringbuf_consume
copies up to
.Fa len
bytes from the buffer into
.Fa dst .
Returns the number of bytes consumed.
.Fn mbt_ringbuf_discard
advances the read pointer by
.Fa len
bytes without copying.
.Pp
.Fn mbt_ringbuf_grow
explicitly grows the buffer to hold at least
.Fa want
more bytes, compacting live data to physical offset 0.
The
.Fa out_delta
parameter receives the old read offset, enabling callers to adjust external
references into the buffer (e.g., send queue entry offsets).
Returns \-ENOBUFS if at maximum capacity, \-ENOMEM on failure, 0 on success.
.Pp
.Fn mbt_ringbuf_shrink
reduces the buffer allocation when live data is well below current capacity.
The buffer shrinks to the smallest power-of-2 that holds
.Fa min_cap
plus the current live data.
If the buffer is empty, it is freed entirely.
This is non-fatal: returns 0 even if shrinking is not beneficial.
Callers with external offsets must adjust them by
.Fa out_delta .
.Pp
.Fn mbt_ringbuf_update_peak
records the current live data size if it exceeds the tracked high-water mark.
.Fn mbt_ringbuf_peak
returns the recorded peak.
Use these to decide when to shrink (e.g.,
when peak has been below half of allocated capacity for N round-trip times).
.Pp
.Fn mbt_ringbuf_write_ptr
returns a pointer to the next contiguous write region and its available length.
After writing, call
.Fn mbt_ringbuf_write_done
with the number of bytes written to advance the write pointer.
Returns NULL if the buffer is not yet allocated or has no space.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr mbt 3 ,
.Xr mbt_conn 3
